---
import EnterpriseDetail from '../../components/enterprise/EnterpriseDetail.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/ComparisonTable.astro';
import ShotFrame from '../../components/ShotFrame.astro';
---

<EnterpriseDetail
  slug="governance"
  seoTitle="Access and Oversight | SAM Enterprise"
  seoDescription="How people sign in to a SAM installation, what roles exist, how project membership works, and what administrators and team leads can see."
  title="Who can do what, and what you can see"
  subtitle="Sign-in through your existing GitHub, Google or GitLab accounts, two levels of roles, and a full history of every agent session in each project."
>
  <h2>Sign-in</h2>
  <p>
    Your installation can offer GitHub, Google and GitLab sign-in, plus one-time email codes for people who
    already have an account. With signup approval on, new accounts wait until a superadmin approves them.
  </p>
  <p>
    SAM does not support SAML or a generic OIDC identity provider yet, so you cannot connect Okta or Entra ID
    directly. See the <a href="/enterprise/#not-yet">not-yet list</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Roles</h2>
  <ComparisonTable
    caption="Two levels of roles: across the installation, and within each project."
    rowHeader="Level"
    columns={['Roles', 'What they control']}
    rows={[
      { label: 'Installation', cells: ['Superadmin, admin, user', 'Approving accounts, integrations, installation-wide credentials, budgets and quotas, admin pages.'] },
      { label: 'Project', cells: ['Owner, admin, maintainer, viewer', 'Membership, project settings, profiles, shared credentials and triggers.'] },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    Invite links create access requests that a project owner or admin approves. SAM shows whether the requester
    has access to the project's repository before you approve.
  </p>
  <ShotFrame
    src="/images/features/sam-collab-members-access.png"
    alt="A project's Members page: an owner and four admins with Transfer ownership and Remove member buttons, two pending access requests (one GitHub verified, one flagged as having no repository access) with Approve and Deny, and an invite link with its expiry and use count."
    caption="Project membership: approve or deny access requests, with a repository-access check on each."
  />

  <h2>Profiles and policies</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Agent profiles</strong> bundle an agent, a model, effort, permission mode, runtime and resource
    request under a name. They belong to a project or a user, and every member of a project can use its profiles.
    They are shared presets rather than restrictions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Project policies</strong> are written rules that every agent in the project receives in its
    instructions, such as "never push to main" or "ask before changing the schema". Agents follow them the way
    they follow any instruction. They are not enforced technical controls.
  </p>

  <h2>What you can see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every chat in a project keeps its messages and each tool call the agent made, and project members can read and comment on them.</li>
    <li>A credential attribution panel shows which triggers, tasks, machines and deployments still run on someone's personal key instead of a shared project credential.</li>
    <li>Administrators get pages for users, costs, AI usage, compute usage, quotas, errors, logs and machines.</li>
    <li>Operations tools that let a superadmin's agent read every user's machines and logs are off by default, and every call is recorded in an audit table.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is no organization level above projects and no export of user actions to a SIEM yet. Both are on the
    <a href="/enterprise/#not-yet">not-yet list</a>.
  </p>
</EnterpriseDetail>
